this is some of the stock that we've got  for the chinese market  mitchell taylor's winery has been  selling australian wine since the 1960s  it retails for approximately a thousand  dollars a bottle  and in the past five years china has  been his main focus  that's the back label there with all the  chinese details  but these bottles can now be three times  as expensive because china has imposed  new tariffs of up to 212 percent on  australian wine  i was absolutely shocked it really did  hit me hard  since may australia has been caught in a  trade dispute with china  it has hit not just the wine industry  but also coal barley  lobster and beef some economists say as  much as six percent of australia's gdp  hangs in the balance it's an attack on  free countries  everywhere the bilateral trade dispute  has gone global  china hawks and lawmakers from across  the political spectrum and around the  world  are taking australia aside the white  house national security council even  tweeted that it would serve australian  wine at its holiday reception  politicians are jumping on china's wine  tariff to draw global attention to what  they see as beijing's bullying and  australia's wine makers hope this new  coalition can help save their industry  it's a great initiative the coalition of  partners  can come together and help us all put  pressure  on the chinese regime  the virus began in china and wuhan  that's i think well understood  this statement is what sparked the  dispute when back in april the  australian government called for an  investigation into the origin of the  coronavirus pandemic  beijing which has suggested that the  virus came from outside china  dispelled the criticism  but this diplomatic tussle is about more  than the virus  china has pointed out several grievances  against australia  like when it banned chinese telecom firm  huawei from its 5g network  because it said it posed a national  security threat even though huawei said  it has operated safely in the country  [Applause]  and when australia condemned china's  controversial national security law in  hong kong  which beijing said is a domestic matter  so in may  china slapped tariffs of up to 80 on  barley followed by levies on other goods  timber exports were also stopped at  chinese customs  for entrepreneurs like mitchell taylor  these tensions threaten their businesses  as they've been big on china and  invested to expand production by buying  new land  and upgrading facilities our current  sales into the chinese market  are about 10 to 15 percent we've been  explaining  our wines and matching them with the  very good  chinese cuisine taylor says sales in  china have doubled over the past four  years  as drinking wine became a status symbol  for the chinese middle class  to appeal to more chinese drinkers  taylor adapted his products  the chinese customers they're fascinated  with  technology we have a microchip here  it is a great security seal  taylor also uses gold on labels which is  perceived as a lucky color in china  he created heavy wooden boxes that he  said chinese customers associate with  exclusivity  and the company even produced wines that  taylor says chinese consumers like to  have with hotpot  taylor's isn't the only winery in  australia to have bet big on china  exports there have tripled in the past  five years to hit 907 million dollars in  2019.  australia's economy as a whole has come  to rely on china  sending a third of its exports to the  mainland  australia has not had an economic  recession  in about two decades and  much of that is thanks to china's rise  viki shu is an analyst who's been  studying australia's increasingly tense  relationship with china  despite the closer economic relationship  between both countries  australia's government has been raising  its voice on political issues sensitive  to beijing  people in the business community are  very anxious here in australia  the current administration is speaking  out assertively on issues like human  rights  but the question is can we really stand  up for our values despite  economic losses we will not be bullied  this trade dispute especially china's  wine tariff has caught the attention of  a new alliance of more than a hundred  lawmakers for more than a dozen  countries  they say trade policies shouldn't be  used as a political tool  we are asking you all to join us in  standing against ching yangping's  authoritarian bullying  by drinking a bottle or two of  australian wine  as trade disputes multiply in a  globalized economy  viki shu says countries that share  political values could increasingly rely  on each other's market  everyone knows they will have help and  they will have backup  when they're also being punished by  beijing for perceived  indiscretion we really do appreciate our  allies and friends coming together  we've got an opportunity now to  re-launch in  the u.s market the uk and in canada  but because consumers there are  different from the chinese taylor  is tweaking the marketing around his  lineup of more than 50 bottles  the labels in chinese we'll have to rip  them off  we'll have to rebottle it and then  repackage it  for the u.s market taylor is using  floral labels to appeal to young urban  consumers  and will start exporting vegan wine to  cities like new york and los  angeles and for the long term  he's planting more shiraz and grenache  which are growing in popularity in the  west  still it'll be difficult to make up for  lost business  some australia-based foreign policy  analysts hope with the incoming biden  administration  like-minded countries could work  together to confront china  as the president-elect has said that he  will seek to strengthen global alliances  the idea that we're going to be able to  deal with china's rogue behavior when we  make up  25 of the world's economy without  bringing in the other 40 percent of our  allies  is not realistic meanwhile china says  it's committed to free trade and that  its tariff measures  are in line with international customary  practices and that it's now up to  australia to repair the relationship  taylor knows he's closing out a tough  2020 without the same access to the  china market  but he says he's learned a lesson for  next year this whole  incident has really taught us  not to get too focused on one customers  we've just got to diversify and do that
 

